{
  "dom_id": "step-q155558",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14178066/assets/c52160535ccb69dd4a70c6e8a172529b599a13e45e3828be7be03da95e35c10c.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $s∥t.$",
  "worked_solution": "Consider $∠1$ and $∠2$ in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14178066/assets/fd9f8d0ecb9bf35fa0a3c8f478a2f25062d37013704d4e1b4e3b0de27c2b91cc.png)\n\nNote that $∠1$ and the angle whose measure is $2x$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠1=2x.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, note that $∠2$ and the angle whose measure is $5x$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠2=5x.\n$$\n\nLet's add these measures to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14178066/assets/fe578ba5253792e812decdab76c76afd31e1d3e2f709bd202b1b0ecda5ef3fc4.png)\n\nThe angles $∠1$, $∠2,$ and the third angle whose measure is ${40}^{∘}$, combine to give a straight angle. So, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠1+m∠2+{40}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2x+5x+{40}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 7x+{40}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 7x & ={140}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{7x}{7} & =\\frac{{140}^{∘}}{7} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{7}x}{\\cancel{7}} & ={20}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={20}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
